\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The single injected dose provides an extraordinary, long-lasting panacea effect, completely neutralizing any potential threat from our airborne or waterborne TMinus weapon by rewriting the target genomic blocks before a bioweapon can bind to them. But eventually, the epigenetic silencing takes over. The code decays, you see. The cell\'92s defences will always win. To keep that perfect, extended lifespan active, subjects require regular, controlled maintenance booster injections to outpace the methylation decay.\'94\
Jansen frowned, his mind tracking the commercial viability of a drug that required a constant needle. \'93Controlled maintenance. You told the board this thing is sexually transmitted. If it escapes the lab, it\'92s a global pandemic. Nobody will buy the boosters if they can just catch it in a bedroom.\'94\
Hermann let out a short, superior scoff. "It is sexually transmitted, but it is mathematically self-limiting. We engineered a strict dilution gradient into the horizontal transmission vectors. The direct injection gives the host the full, pristine protection. If that client transmits it sexually to a partner\'97a first degree of separation\'97the partner receives a heavily degraded viral titer. The efficacy drops by half. It might cure their baseline illnesses, but it won't extend their lifespan by eighty years."\
Jansen\'92s brow furrowed. "And if that partner sleeps with someone else? A second degree?"\
"Then it is a completely inert shadow," Hermann said triumphantly, waving a hand dismissively toward the air vents. "The viral load at the second degree of separation is so mathematically miniscule that the host\'92s baseline methylation systems chew it up and silence it within minutes. It is a biological dead end. The virus physically cannot survive three links down the transmission chain. If the public wants the miracle, they still have to come to us for the needle."\
Jansen shifted his feet, looking at the technical array monitors. "What about uncontrolled maintenance? What if someone tries to force the issue? Dumps a whole bucket of the stuff into their system to make it stick?"\
Hermann scoffed. \'93A bucket of my stuff or even a concerted, high-dose regimen would simply accelerate systemic organ failure. The human body does not passively tolerate prolonged genomic intrusion. If you maintain a maximum viral volume over months, the host cells respond by up-regulating their epigenetic defences, aggressively accelerating the methylation rate to crush the virus. Push past that defensive limit with even higher doses, and you trigger a terminal cellular checkpoint. The immune system recognises the persistent, massive foreign load and undergoes catastrophic anaphylaxis. The organs fail completely long before the virus can achieve chromosomal integration.\'94\
\'93So there\'92s no risk of it becoming a permanent rewrite?\'94\
\'93Permanent integration requires illegitimate recombination\'97the episomal virus accidentally splicing into a host chromosome," Hermann said, his tone shifting into the flat rhythm of scientific absolute certainty. "That mechanical error only occurs if the host cell suffers double-stranded DNA breaks at the exact microsecond its repair enzymes, like ligase IV, are completely saturated by an overwhelming viral volume. It is a statistical absurdity. A viral concentration high enough to saturate those repair pathways triggers immediate, systemic toxic shock. The kidneys liquefy, the immune system undergoes catastrophic anaphylaxis, and the host dies within minutes. The biological engine fails completely long before the genetic threshold is reached.\'94\
Jansen slowly let out his breath, his eyes dropping to the digital clipboards detailing the operational schedule. \'93And we are currently entering Phase IIa trials, correct? Target population dose-ranging?\'94\
\'93Yes, yes,\'94 Hermann said impatiently, adjusting the dial on a digital incubator. \'93The safety data is locked from Phase I. We are demonstrating proof of concept to the board this quarter before we even look at a production rollout timeline. We are proving that this platform makes a human being functionally\'97for want of a better word\'97invincible to both natural disease and our own TMinus architecture. It extends the active, disease-free lifespan out to a century and a half. Unless, I guess, you fall from a window.\'94\
Egan Jansen didn't chortle. He stared at the glowing green glass, thinking of Jamison Hardy\'92s massive stakes in global pharmaceuticals, private medical infrastructure, and generational real estate portfolios. Even with the dilution gradient limiting its horizontal spread, a virus that could nullify their primary weapon, spread across a marriage bed, and extend lives for a hundred and sixty years was a nightmare. Releasing it would dismantle the entire socioeconomic machine.\
It wasn't a product to be sold. It was a structural demolition charge.\
\'93Thank you, Doctor,\'94 Jansen said, turning toward the cleanroom exit decontamination airlock. \'93I have exactly what Mr. Hardy needs to hear.\'94\
